What is a kernel module ?

	A kernel module is loadable kernel code. 

	It : 

		* Runs in kernel space 
		* Extends kernel functionality 
		* Can be loaded/unloaded at runtime 

	Commands: 

		insmod module.ko
		rmmod module
		modprobe module

	Unlike user applications, modules : 

		* Do not have main()
		* Do not use libc 
		* Do not have automatic cleanup 


Hello World Module Structure 

	#include <linux/module.h>
	#include <linux/init.h>

	MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");

	static int __init hello_init(void) {

		printk(KERN_ALERT "Hello World\n"); 
		return 0;		
	}

	static void __exit hello_exit(void) {

		printk(KERN_ALERT "Goodbye\n");
	}

	module_init(hello_init);
	module_exit(hello_exit);

	
	Important components 

		* MODULE_LICENSE 

		  Declares module license. 

			* Required to avoid kernel taint 
			* Some kernel symbols only available to GPL modules 

		* module_init()

			Defines function called when: 

				 insmod module.ko 

			equvalent to program start - but not exactly main()

		* module_exit()

			Called during : 

				 rmmod module 

			unlike user applications : 

				No automatic cleanup 
				If we forget to free memory, it stays allocated


4. Tainted Kernel 

	A kernel becomes tainter when : 

		* Non GPL module is loaded 
		* Out-of-tree module is loaded
		* Certain unsafe operations occur 

	Meaning : 

		Kernel developers may refuse to debug issues

	Check taint status : 

		cat /proc/sys/kernel/tainted 


5. Module vs Application 


	| Feature        | User Application | Kernel Module    |
	| -------------- | ---------------- | ---------------- |
	| Entry function | main()           | module_init()    |
	| Exit function  | return           | module_exit()    |
	| Uses libc      | Yes              | No               |
	| printf()       | Yes              | No               |
	| printk()       | No               | Yes              |
	| Stack size     | ~2 MB            | ~4 KB            |
	| Floating point | Allowed          | Not supported    |
	| Crash impact   | Kills process    | May crash system |


6. Kernel Stack Limitations 

	Kernel stack size : 

		~4KB (architecture dependent)

		All nested function calls share this stack. 
		
		Therfore: 

			* Do not declare large local arrays
			* Use dynamic allocation (kmalloc)
			* Avoid deep recursion 


7. Linking a Module into Kernel 

	When using insmod : 

		* Module is loaded into memory 
		* Symbols are resolved against kernel symbol table 
		* Linked in memory (not disk)
		* Unresolved symbols cause failure 

