8. Kernel Entry Mechanisms 
	
	Kernel can be entered in 2 ways : 

		Kernel can be entered in 2 ways: 

			1. System calls 

			Triggered by user space : 

				read()
				write()
				ioctl()

			Flow : 

				User -> System Call Handler -> Driver -> Hardware 

			Synchronous 

	
			2. Interrupts  

			Triggered by hardware : 

				* Network packet 
				* Keyboard press 
				* Timer interrupt 

			Flow : 

				Hardware -> Interrupt -> Driver -> Kernel 

			Asynchronous 


9. User Space vs Kernel Space 

	Modern processors enforce : 

	* Protection levels 
	* Seperate address spaces 
	* Privilege seperation
	
	User Space 

	* Lowest privilege level 
	* Cannot access hardware directly 
	* Cannot access kernel memory 
	* Crashes do not affect system

	Kernel Space 

	* Highest privilege (supervisor mode)
	* Full hardware access 
	* Full memory accesss 
	* Entered via syscall or interrupt

		Our driver runs here  


10. Concurrency in Kernel 

	Kernel supports: 

		* Multiple processes 
		* Asynchronous interrupts 
		* SMP (multiple CPUs)
	
	Therefore: 
		
		Our driver must be : Reentrant 

	What does reentrant mean ?

		* Multiple threads may enter your driver simultaneously 
		* Data structures must be protected 
		* Shared resources must be synchronized 

		Even if your code never sleeps: 

			Interrupts can preempt execution 
			Other CPUs may run your driver concurrently 


11. Why Concurrency is Hard in Kernel ?

	Because : 

		* No automatic memory isolation 
		* Interrupt context differs from process context 
		* Must handle : 

			* Race conditions 
			* Deadlocks 
			* Priority inversion 

		   This leads into spinlocks, mutexes, etc
















































































		














































	





















